hvf: latch an SPI's rising edge as pending in the kernel VGIC.

Track M24.  Under HVF with Apple's VGIC (kernel-irqchip, the default on
the virt board) MSIs go through GICv2m, which turns each into a pulse:
hv_gic_set_spi(intid, 1) and at once (intid, 0).  A pulse that rises and
falls before the VGIC samples the line is lost.  Windows 11 on Arm with
viogpudo taking the host's vertical blank (patch 90, an MSI 60 times a
second) froze within minutes: three vCPUs asleep in the VGIC's WFI for
good.  Each rising edge now also sets the SPI's GICD_ISPENDR bit (write
one to set), so the interrupt stays pending until the guest takes it; a
level device is still asserted when the guest does.

diff --git a/hw/intc/arm_gicv3_hvf.c b/hw/intc/arm_gicv3_hvf.c
index ae88109..80e83d6 100644
--- a/hw/intc/arm_gicv3_hvf.c
+++ b/hw/intc/arm_gicv3_hvf.c
@@ -600,10 +600,22 @@ static void hvf_gicv3_get(GICv3State *s)
 static void hvf_gicv3_set_irq(void *opaque, int irq, int level)
 {
     GICv3State *s = opaque;
+    uint32_t intid = GIC_INTERNAL + irq;
+
     if (irq > s->num_irq) {
         return;
     }
-    hv_gic_set_spi(GIC_INTERNAL + irq, !!level);
+    hv_gic_set_spi(intid, !!level);
+    if (level) {
+        /*
+         * Latch the rising edge as pending too: GICv2m turns each MSI into
+         * a pulse (raise, then lower at once), and a pulse that rises and
+         * falls before the kernel VGIC samples the line is lost.  A level
+         * device is still asserted when the guest takes it.
+         */
+        hv_gic_set_distributor_reg(HV_GIC_DISTRIBUTOR_REG_GICD_ISPENDR0 +
+                                   (intid / 32) * 4, 1u << (intid % 32));
+    }
 }
 
 static void hvf_gicv3_icc_reset(CPUARMState *env, const ARMCPRegInfo *ri)
